# <%= current_page.data.title %>

This covers how your application reaches production, and the steps to take before and after a deployment.

## Before your first production deployment

### Get your repository approved for production

To allow Jenkins to deploy to production, add your GitHub repository to the `prod` list in the approved repositories file:

- CFT: [hmcts/cnp-jenkins-config/environment-approvals.yml](https://github.com/hmcts/cnp-jenkins-config/blob/master/environment-approvals.yml)
- SDS: [hmcts/sds-jenkins-config/environment-approvals.yml](https://github.com/hmcts/sds-jenkins-config/blob/master/environment-approvals.yml)

Production is the only environment that requires approval. If your repository is not on the list, the pipeline skips the production stages and prints a warning banner in the build log instead — see [approvedEnvironmentRepository](https://github.com/hmcts/cnp-jenkins-library/blob/master/vars/approvedEnvironmentRepository.groovy).

### Check your team configuration

Build and deployment notifications go to the Slack channels registered for your product in [team-config.yml](https://github.com/hmcts/cnp-jenkins-config/blob/master/team-config.yml) (CFT) or the equivalent file in [sds-jenkins-config](https://github.com/hmcts/sds-jenkins-config) (SDS). Check that `contact_channel`, `channel_id` and `build_notices_channel` are correct for your team — [alerting](alerting.html) uses these values too.

### Prepare your production configuration

- **Infrastructure**: if your repository has an `infrastructure` folder, the pipeline runs Terraform against the production subscription on merges to `master`, so your production variables must exist. Pull requests that change infrastructure also run a Terraform plan against production, unless the pull request has the `not-plan-on-prod` label or topic — see [withPipeline](https://github.com/hmcts/cnp-jenkins-library/blob/master/vars/withPipeline.groovy).
- **Flux configuration**: add a production overlay for your application — `apps/<team>/<component>/prod.yaml` in [cnp-flux-config](https://github.com/hmcts/cnp-flux-config) (CFT) or [sds-flux-config](https://github.com/hmcts/sds-flux-config) (SDS) — with your production ingress host, Key Vault secrets, environment variables and scaling values. The [plum prod overlay](https://github.com/hmcts/cnp-flux-config/blob/master/apps/cnp/plum-fastapi-backend/prod.yaml) from the platform's test service is a worked example.
- **Secrets**: any Key Vault secrets your production configuration references must exist in the production vault before the first deployment.
- **Database changes**: if your pipeline enables database migrations (`enableDbMigration` in your Jenkinsfile), the migration runs as a pipeline stage in each environment, including production. Read the [database schema change standards](/cloud-native-platform/standards/db-schema-change.html) before you rely on this for production changes.

### Operational readiness

Before go-live, make sure:

- [Health probes](health-endpoints.html) are configured.
- [Monitoring](monitoring.html) is in place.
- [Alerting](alerting.html) is in place.
- [Shuttering](shutter.html) is configured and tested, for user-facing services.
- Your release is sized correctly and, if needed, an [Operational Acceptance Test](oat.html) is arranged — see [Release Categorisation](/cloud-native-platform/guides/release-sizing.html).

## What happens when you deploy

A merge to `master` runs the stages below, in this order — see [withPipeline](https://github.com/hmcts/cnp-jenkins-library/blob/master/vars/withPipeline.groovy):

1. Build and test.
2. Infrastructure build and optional database migration against staging (AAT).
3. Deploy to the staging cluster — smoke tests and functional tests run here.
4. The Helm chart is published.
5. Infrastructure build and optional database migration against production. This is gated by the environment approvals above, and a lock allows only one production deployment per application at a time.
6. The Docker image that passed every stage is promoted: it is re-tagged in the container registry as `prod-<commit>-<timestamp>` — see [sectionPromoteBuildToStage](https://github.com/hmcts/cnp-jenkins-library/blob/master/vars/sectionPromoteBuildToStage.groovy). Older production tags are purged at the same time.

Pipelines can also run optional stages, such as high level data setup, where a repository configures them.

The production rollout itself is GitOps, not a pipeline stage. Flux watches the container registry: when a new production tag appears, the image automation commits the new tag to the Flux configuration repository (the commit message is the image reference — you can see these on the repository's `master` branch), and the Flux controllers then upgrade your application on the production clusters. The `# {"$imagepolicy": ...}` marker comment next to the image in your `prod.yaml` is what allows the automation to rewrite the tag.

Two consequences of this are worth understanding:

- **A green Jenkins build does not mean the rollout has finished.** Image promotion is the last pipeline step; the production rollout happens asynchronously after it.
- **The pipeline does not run smoke tests against production.** Smoke and functional tests run in preview and staging only, so you need to verify production yourself after each release.

## After a deployment

- Confirm the image update commit has appeared in the Flux configuration repository — the commit message is the image tag you expect.
- Confirm your application upgraded and is healthy. Deployment failures in your namespace are posted to your team's Slack channel — see [alerting](alerting.html).
- Check your dashboards and logs for errors and unusual patterns — see [monitoring](monitoring.html).

### Rolling back

The Flux image policy always selects the newest production-tagged image, and older tags are purged, so you cannot roll back by deleting a tag or re-deploying an old one. To roll back application code, revert the change in GitHub and let the pipeline build and promote a new image from the reverted code.

Database changes need their own rollback plan — a reverted application image does not undo a schema migration. See the [database schema change standards](/cloud-native-platform/standards/db-schema-change.html).
